trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Neurologic exam is entirely normal — no focal deficits, no ataxia, intact cognition and cranial nerves. This strongly argues against seizure, stroke, TIA, or structural neurologic disease as cause of syncope. Combined with normal orthostatics, normal cardiac exam, absence of comorbidities/medications, and classic temporal association with micturition, the clinical picture is highly consistent with reflex (vasovagal) syncope — specifically micturition syncope. However, Domain 8 (most informative lab/imaging/microbiology/pathology) remains unaddressed. To confirm or exclude life-threatening mimics, essential tests include:  
- ECG (to detect arrhythmia, conduction disease, QT prolongation, ischemia) — *mandatory in all syncope evaluations*;  
- Troponin (if concern for ACS, though low pretest probability here);  
- CBC, electrolytes, glucose (to rule out anemia, hyponatremia, hypoglycemia).  

ECG is the single highest-yield, most discriminating test in syncope — it can diagnose Brugada, LQTS, AV block, VT, or acute MI that would change management immediately. It is universally available in ED.

[TEST] 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG).

